<BaseLayout title="A thoughtful plan for one small living room" description="Explore a conceptual 3 by 4 meter living room with resting, hiding, scratching and play zones, plus clear limits for adapting the plan.">
<article class="container article-container"><p class="eyebrow"><a href="/notebook/">THE NOTEBOOK</a> / SMALL SPACE, BETTER DAYS</p><h1>A little room.<br/>More thoughtful choices.</h1><p class="lead">An original planning example for a 3 × 4 m living room (about 9.8 × 13.1 ft). Start with existing furniture and keep the center useful.</p><p class="resource-date">Mew &amp; Maple · Planning example · October 18, 2026</p><div class="quick-answer"><h2>The idea in a minute</h2><p>Give the room more than one purpose: a place to watch, retreat, scratch and play. Keep paths open and check the rest of the home before treating one room as a complete setup.</p></div>
<figure class="room-figure"><img src="/notebook/small-room-plan.svg" alt="Conceptual top-down diagram of a 3 by 4 meter living room with five numbered zones" width="640" height="620"/><figcaption>Original editorial diagram. Room dimensions are stated; furniture sizes and positions are illustrative. This is not a photographed or safety-tested installation.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="article-prose"><h2>Read the five zones</h2><ol><li><strong>Watch and rest.</strong> A stable, reachable resting place near a closed window. Select support and access for the individual cat; follow product installation instructions.</li><li><strong>Retreat.</strong> A simple hideaway beside existing furniture, with an unobstructed way out. Avoid making the exit part of a busy doorway.</li><li><strong>Scratch.</strong> A stable surface near a route the cat already uses. Confirm the actual footprint and that it does not block people or doors.</li><li><strong>Play.</strong> A flexible patch of open floor for supervised interaction. Store strand-like toys and loose pieces after use.</li><li><strong>Move freely.</strong> An open route through the room, including the door's actual swing. The dashed route shows intent, not a measured clearance standard.</li></ol>
<h2>What this plan leaves elsewhere</h2><p>This is one living room within a larger home. Food, water and litter resources must also be accessible in appropriate separated locations. The diagram does not assess those locations or establish that the whole home meets a cat's needs. It is not a multi-cat resource plan.</p>
<h2>Adapt before you copy</h2><ul><li>Measure the real room, furniture, door swings, heaters and accessible windows.</li><li>Check reach and routes for your cat's age, confidence and mobility.</li><li>Use existing furniture only when its stability and use are suitable. A diagram cannot certify an attachment or load limit.</li><li>Try one change, watch voluntary use and adjust. Do not force a cat into a new resting place.</li></ul>
<h2>One thoughtful change this week</h2><p>Sketch the route your cat already uses. Choose one resting or scratching opportunity along it and check that your change keeps access open. Use the <a href="/notebook/#home-checklist">home checklist</a> to review what lies outside this room.</p><h2>Sources behind the approach</h2><p>The plan is our own editorial example. Its emphasis on safe places, separated resources and play draws on the <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11383066/">AAFP/ISFM environmental guidelines</a>. Toy cautions draw on <a href="https://www.vet.cornell.edu/departments-centers-and-institutes/cornell-feline-health-center/health-information/feline-health-topics/safe-toys-and-gifts">Cornell's toy guidance</a>. Neither source evaluated or endorsed this diagram.</p></div><div class="article-end"><a href="/notebook/">Explore the free Notebook resources →</a></div></article></BaseLayout>
